\toprule
\textbf{Testing Pair Comparison} & \textbf{Key Methodological and Architectural Distinction} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Vulnerability Assessment vs. Penetration Testing} & Automated scanning to identify and catalog weaknesses vs. authorized exploitation to prove impact. \\
\textbf{SAST vs. DAST} & Static source-code analysis without execution vs. runtime black-box testing of a running application. \\
\textbf{Black-Box vs. White-Box} & Zero internal implementation knowledge vs. full access to source code, architecture, and database schemas. \\
\textbf{White-Box vs. Gray-Box} & Complete transparent internal access vs. partial internal knowledge (e.g., standard user credentials). \\
\textbf{Security Testing vs. Functional Testing} & Verifying resilience against intentional malicious misuse vs. verifying conformance to business requirements. \\
\textbf{Functional PIN vs. Security PIN Testing} & Verifying that valid PINs authenticate successfully vs. verifying PIN masking, rate limiting, and transport security. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\subsection{Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Cybersecurity}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Master Technical Comparison Matrix: AI and Machine Learning Concepts}
\label{tab:aiml_contrasts_master}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{p{4.5cm} X}
\toprule
\textbf{AI/ML Concept Pair} & \textbf{Key Technical and Algorithmic Distinction} \\
\midrule
\textbf{AI vs. Machine Learning} & Broad computational systems simulating human cognition vs. algorithms learning statistical patterns from data. \\
\textbf{ML vs. Deep Learning} & Statistical tabular/feature models (Random Forest, SVM) vs. multi-layered neural networks (CNNs, Transformers). \\
\textbf{Supervised vs. Unsupervised} & Trained on explicit labeled datasets $(x_i, y_i)$ vs. discovering latent geometric clusters in unlabelled data. \\
\textbf{Classification vs. Clustering} & Assigning samples into predefined target classes vs. grouping unlabeled samples based on statistical similarity. \\
\textbf{False Positive vs. False Negative} & Legitimate benign activity flagged as an attack (SOC fatigue) vs. genuine attack missed (security breach). \\
\textbf{Precision vs. Recall} & Proportion of generated alerts that are truly attacks vs. proportion of total existing attacks detected. \\
\textbf{Evasion vs. Data Poisoning} & Manipulating inference inputs to fool a trained model vs. corrupting the training dataset during model training. \\
\textbf{Model Extraction vs. Membership Inference} & Querying an API to clone proprietary model weights vs. determining if a specific record was in the training set. \\
\textbf{Traditional vs. AI Cybersecurity} & Static signature matching (deterministic, blind to zero-days) vs. statistical anomaly detection (adaptive, opaque). \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

